yield: for a 2kg boneless shoulder — serves 6-8 sandwiches
uses:
  - sourdough
  - slaw
  - quick-pickles
ingredients:
  - item: pork shoulder
    mass: 2000
    basis: true
    note: boneless, skin off, fat cap trimmed to ~5mm; bone-in works, add ~1hr
components:
  - name: rub
    ingredients:
      - item: cooking salt
        mass: 20
        note: 1% of meat weight; kosher salt — reduce 25% for table salt, increase 50% for flakes
      - item: brown sugar
        mass: 40
        qty: 2
        unit: tbsp
        note: sub white
      - item: smoked paprika
        mass: 15
        qty: 2
        unit: tbsp
        note: sub sweet paprika
      - item: garlic powder
        mass: 9
        qty: 1
        unit: tbsp
      - item: onion powder
        mass: 6
        qty: 2
        unit: tsp
      - item: mustard powder
        mass: 5.5
        qty: 2
        unit: tsp
      - item: cumin powder
        mass: 4
        qty: 2
        unit: tsp
      - item: coarse black pepper
        mass: 4
        qty: 1
        unit: tbsp
      - item: cayenne
        mass: 0.4
        qty: 0.5
        unit: tsp
        optional: true
        note: for spice
    steps:
      - salt first, then the rest of the mix — all faces, into the crevices
  - name: mop
    ingredients:
      - item: apple cider vinegar
        mass: 120
      - item: apple cider
        mass: 120
        note: or apple juice
      - item: liquid smoke
        mass: 4
        optional: true
        note: hickory; apple-wood to harmonise with the cider. see notes — hold 2g back for the finishing sauce
    steps:
      - combine in a sprayer
  - name: finishing sauce (carolina vinegar)
    ingredients:
      - item: apple cider vinegar
        mass: 240
      - item: water
        mass: 60
      - item: tomato sauce
        mass: 60
      - item: brown sugar
        mass: 30
      - item: salt
        mass: 5
      - item: chili flakes
        mass: 2
        qty: 1
        unit: tsp
      - item: black pepper
        mass: 2
        qty: 1
        unit: tsp
      - item: hot sauce
        optional: true
        note: few dashes
      - item: liquid smoke
        mass: 2
        optional: true
        note: the 2g held back from the mop — add by drops, tasting
    steps:
      - whisk everything cold until sugar and salt dissolve — no cooking needed
      - rest at least 30 mins for the chili to bloom
  - name: procedure (oven)
    steps:
      - pat dry, apply rub, refrigerate uncovered overnight (4 hrs minimum)
      - out of fridge 1 hr before cooking
      - oven to 115-120°C fan-forced, or 135°C conventional — fan is the better cook, see notes
      - wire rack over a drip tray; leave the tray dry for maximum bark, or fill it with water as insurance against a dry exterior
      - fat cap up, uncovered, until core hits 70-75°C and the bark is set and dark (3-4 hrs)
      - spray with mop every hour once the surface looks dry
      - wrap tight in foil (or butcher paper) with a splash of mop inside
      - double layer, seams folded up on top, foil tight against the meat with minimal airspace, parcel sat in a tray in case it weeps
      - 'leave-in probe: bring the cable out through the top seam and crimp the foil around it — liquid pools at the bottom, so a top exit only loses steam'
      - don't pull the probe to make wrapping easier — every hole is a leak path, and re-siting it blind risks landing in a fat seam that lies about the core temp
      - keep the tray drippings, but taste them first — see notes
      - deglaze the still-warm tray with a splash of apple juice or stock, scrape the fond in
      - back in until core reaches 96°C and a skewer slides in with no resistance (another 3-4 hrs)
      - rest wrapped 1 hr — this is not optional, it reabsorbs the juices
      - unwrap over a bowl, keep the rendered juices
      - pull with two forks or claws, discarding hard fat and gristle
      - toss the pulled meat with the reserved juices first, then finishing sauce to taste
  - name: procedure (slow-cooker)
    steps:
      - rub as above, sear all faces hard in a pan first (the slow cooker won't build bark)
      - 8 hrs on low with 100g of the mop in the bottom, no other liquid
      - lift out, pull, then reduce the cooking liquid in a pan before tossing it through
    notes: |
      Easier, but you trade the bark for it. Worth spreading the pulled meat on a tray and
      blasting under a hot grill for 5-10 mins to get some crisp edges back.
  - name: holding (if it's done early)
    ingredients:
      - item: chicken or pork stock
        mass: 100
        note: lightly salted, ~1%, to top up the wrap. salted apple juice also works — not the mop, see notes
    steps:
      - rest wrapped on the bench for the usual 1 hr first — don't go from 90°C straight into a warm oven, the rest only works while the temperature is falling
      - hold it whole and wrapped, never shredded
      - up to ~3 hrs — wrapped roast plus towels into a pre-warmed esky, sits around 60-70°C, gentlest option
      - longer than that — oven at 65°C, topping up the liquid in the wrap
      - past ~4 hrs — consider cooling it properly and reheating gently in its own juices instead
      - shred at the last minute; get the toast going before the meat comes out of the wrap
    notes: |
      Shred late, always. Shredded meat has enormous surface area and dries and cools fast,
      where a whole wrapped roast holds almost indefinitely. It's also mechanically easier —
      meat that has cooled firms up and shreds worse.

      60°C is the safety line: above it you're fine more or less forever, below it you're in
      the danger zone. An esky drifts down, so if the hold creeps past 3 hrs move it to the
      oven rather than hoping.

      Top up with lightly salted stock, or salted apple juice — not the mop. The mop is half
      cider vinegar, which is fine spraying onto an open surface where it mostly evaporates,
      but sealed in a wrap for hours the acid keeps working and the exterior goes mushy and
      over-tangy. Salt is what helps a lean cut hold moisture; acid does nothing for it. You
      also reclaim this liquid and toss it through at the end, so you want it tasting like
      pork jus — the vinegar comes later, in the finishing sauce, where you can taste as you
      go.

      Cool-and-reheat over a long hot hold is counterintuitive but sound: time at temperature
      is what dries lean meat, and pulled or braised cuts reheat exceptionally well since
      they're already broken down and sitting in liquid. It's fiddlier and passes the danger
      zone twice, so it's only worth it past about four hours.
  - name: variant (leg instead of shoulder)
    ingredients:
      - item: salt
        mass: 100
        note: for a 5% wet brine in 2L water, 12-24 hrs, replacing the dry brine
      - item: butter
        mass: 50
        note: at the pull, not the wrap — see notes
      - item: salted apple juice
        mass: 100
        note: the main wrap liquid, lightly salted only. or stock
      - item: lard or bacon fat
        mass: 30
        optional: true
        note: into the foil at the wrap
    steps:
      - wet brine 12-24 hrs at 5% instead of the overnight dry brine — this is the change that matters most
      - if there's no time to brine, inject with salted apple juice or stock instead; it works in minutes, but only while the meat is still raw
      - once it's up to temp it's too late to inject — the muscle is already contracting and squeezing liquid out, so put that liquid in the foil at the wrap instead
      - leave the fat cap thick and untrimmed, cap up
      - keep water in the drip tray — with a lean cut, take the humidity over the bark
      - wrap early, at 65°C rather than 70-75°C
      - 'into the foil: 100g salted apple juice as the main liquid, only a small splash of mop, and 30g lard or bacon fat if you have it'
      - go light on the mop here — wrapping early and then holding means hours sealed, and that's too long for something half cider vinegar (same reasoning as the hold top-up)
      - keep the salt light in the juice; the rub is already on and the finishing sauce brings more
      - pull at 90-92°C, NOT 96°C
      - expect 5-6 hrs for 2kg rather than 7-9
      - there'll be less rendered juice, so toss with 50g melted butter at the pull and lean harder on the finishing sauce
      - better than the butter if the tray drippings came out clean — use the fat lifted off those instead
    notes: |
      A compromise, not an equal. Leg runs ~8-10% intramuscular fat against shoulder's 20%+,
      and its collagen sits in sheets between the muscle groups rather than webbed all the
      way through, so it shreds coarser — sliceable-shreddable rather than fluffy.

      If the leg has skin on, consider not pulling it at all. Roasted and sliced with
      crackling is a better dinner than a compromised pulled pork.
  - name: assembly
    steps:
      - thick slices of sourdough, toasted hard — it has to hold up to wet pork
      - butter the toast while hot
      - pile on pork, pickles over the pork, slaw over the top so it doesn't steam
      - open-faced, or top slice on for a proper sandwich
notes: |
  Shoulder (aka pork butt / boston butt) by default. Leg or loin have nowhere near the
  collagen or intramuscular fat, and cooked to these temps as written they go stringy and
  dry — see the leg variant component if that's what you've got.

  Drippings are worth reclaiming, but taste them before you commit. If the tray ran dry the
  rub can scorch — there's 40g of brown sugar in it and the tray takes direct radiant heat.
  Dark brown and rich, keep it; black or acrid, bin it, because it will poison the whole
  batch and there's no getting it back out.

  If they're good, they beat butter for finishing. Pour them into a container and refrigerate
  while the meat rests or holds: the fat sets into a disc you lift straight off, leaving
  concentrated jus underneath. Melt that rendered pork fat through the meat at the pull —
  right flavour, pure fat with none of butter's water, and it came out of the same animal.
  Deglazing the warm tray gets you the fond as well, which is the most concentrated flavour
  in the whole cook.

  Don't tip drippings into the wrap. The fat just pools, and you'd be adding liquid to a
  pouch whose level you're already managing. Reclaim it separately and use it at the pull.

  Where the fat goes, for a lean cut: at the pull, overwhelmingly. Fat does not penetrate
  meat. Muscle is about 75% water and melted butter cannot migrate into the fibres, so
  anything you put in the foil is coating the outside and pooling, not moisturising the
  interior. What actually makes lean pulled pork feel dry is bare strands in the mouth with
  nothing coating them, and that's a problem you can only fix after shredding, when the
  surface area is enormous and every strand takes the fat.

  Some fat at the wrap is still worth it, but for a different reason: it turns the wrap
  liquid into a fatty braise rather than a thin acidic one, and you reclaim that liquid and
  toss it through at the end anyway. Use lard or bacon fat there rather than butter —
  butter is 16-18% water and milk solids, so you're adding less fat than you think, and it
  reads oddly against the vinegar sauce. Save the butter for the pull, where you can taste
  as you go.

  The target isn't doneness, it's collagen breakdown. 96°C core held there long enough is
  what turns connective tissue to gelatin; a shoulder pulled at 85°C is cooked but won't
  shred. Go by probe feel over the thermometer at the end — no resistance anywhere.

  The stall around 70-75°C is evaporative cooling, not a problem. Wrapping pushes through
  it and stops the bark going leathery. Foil is faster and softens the bark; butcher paper
  is slower and keeps it crisper.

  Fan-forced or conventional: fan, at 115-120°C. It only matters for the unwrapped phase —
  once it's in foil there's no surface evaporation and airflow does nothing, so run whatever
  you like after the wrap.

  Two reasons fan wins on the first phase. Home ovens hold a low conventional setting badly,
  cycling hard with a top-down radiant bias, and over six hours that means one end of the
  shoulder finishes well ahead of the other; the fan gives an even, stable chamber. And bark
  is surface dehydration plus Maillard, which is precisely what moving air does — it's why
  offset smokers and pellet grills out-bark a still oven.

  On the temperature drop: the usual "fan runs 20°C hotter" rule is calibrated for baking at
  180-200°C. Down at 135°C the real offset is nearer 10-15°C, so 115-120°C fan is the
  equivalent, not 115 flat.

  The trade is a worse stall. More airflow means more evaporation means more evaporative
  cooling, so fan deepens and lengthens it — phase 1 will run past 3-4 hrs. Go by the
  70-75°C reading and the look of the bark, not the clock.

  Conventional-only oven: 135°C as written is fine. Rotate the tray every couple of hours
  and accept a patchier bark.

  The drip tray is a genuine trade-off, not an oversight. Water in it stops the drippings
  scorching and humidifies the chamber, which protects the exterior — but humidity is the
  enemy of bark. Dry tray for bark, water for insurance.

  Carolina vinegar sauce over a thick sweet bbq sauce, deliberately — the pork is already
  rich and the toast is already carby. Acid is what makes the sandwich eat well instead of
  sitting on you. Same reason the slaw is sharp.

  Liquid smoke: optional, and dosed differently to pork-ribs. Pulling redistributes it.
  Liquid smoke is a surface treatment — on a rack it stays in the layer you bite through,
  concentrated, but shredding a shoulder mixes that surface through the whole 2kg. More even
  than the ribs ever get, but weaker for the same dose.

  Don't scale the ribs dose by weight. Ribs run 3.5g into 60g water for a 650g rack (5.4g/kg);
  straight-scaled that's ~11g here, which is far too much. A rack is flat with enormous
  surface per gram, a shoulder is a blocky roast. You're dosing surface area, not mass.

  So: 4g into the mop, 2g held back for the finishing sauce. The sauce is the real control
  point — the meat gets tossed in it anyway, so it distributes perfectly and you can taste
  as you go. Liquid smoke turns acrid and ashtray fast with no way back, so arrive under and
  add drops.

  Not in the rub — it's water-based, it turns the rub pasty and works against the surface
  drying that builds bark.

  No curing salt, unlike pork-ribs. That's there for the pink smoke ring, and this gets
  shredded, so nobody will ever see one. Dropping it also removes the ribs recipe's
  constraint on resting overnight, which this wants anyway for the uncovered dry brine.

  If you own a kettle or smoker, do it properly instead. Phase 1 — unwrapped, surface still
  damp — is the smoke absorption window; meat largely stops taking smoke once the surface
  dries and you wrap. Liquid smoke substitutes for not having that, it doesn't equal it.

  Faster oven route: 160°C covered for ~5 hrs. Fine, but you lose most of the bark.
